        "url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/DckX0_pob7k/",
        "caption": "1. If your only skill is manual testing, start considering test automation if for no reason learn it for when you’d need it cos you will\n\n2. Testing is not about finding bugs that’s just 10% of the job, improving processes and preventing bugs is 90%\n\n3. If developers discover more bugs than QA before release, it shows a huge product knowledge gap that needs to be tightened \nTesters who don’t understand the product will always be outperformed by those who do. It’s not always a skill issue\n\n4. I hope you understand shift-left testing: it’s simply “the earlier the better”.\n\n5. You don’t need to know every automation tool. You need to master one and understand testing deeply.\n\n6. Truly, AI can generate test cases in seconds. It still can’t replace critical thinking and domain knowledge.\n\n7. Don’t skip regressions test\n\n8. Quality is everyone’s responsibility. If QA is the only team thinking about quality, your engineering culture is unsustainable.\n\n9. The most influential testers aren’t the ones who click the fastest, they’re the ones who influence product decisions before a single line of code is written.\n\n10. Bug-free software doesn’t exist. Stop promising it. Same as 100% automation\n\n11. Understanding the business impact of a bug further deepens your knowledge of the product and helps you determine the bug severity \n\n12. Risk based testing over Quality/speed anytime\n\n13. I’m yet to find an accurate KPM for QAs tell me how you measure QAs KPIs\n\n#theqagirl #QA #facts #fyppppppppppppppppppppppp #tester \n\nSoftware testing, QA, facts, AI, Remote Work\n\nSave this for later",
