        "shortcode": "Dcup3H3uuqf",
        "url": "https://www.instagram.com/reel/Dcup3H3uuqf/",
        "caption": "1️⃣ Identify the Slow Query\nFirst, measure where the latency is coming from using Spring Boot Actuator, application logs, APM tools, or Hibernate SQL logs. Check API response time and database execution time separately to confirm that the database query is actually the bottleneck.\n\n2️⃣ Analyze the SQL Query\nOnce the query is identified, run it directly against the database and use EXPLAIN / EXPLAIN ANALYZE to understand the execution plan. Look for full table scans, inefficient joins, excessive rows scanned, or expensive sorting/grouping operations.\n\n3️⃣ Optimize Indexes\nCheck whether columns used in WHERE, JOIN, ORDER BY, and frequently used search conditions have appropriate indexes. Adding the right indexes can significantly reduce the number of rows the database needs to scan. However, avoid unnecessary indexes because they increase storage and write overhead.\n\n4️⃣ Optimize the JPA/Hibernate Query\nReview the repository method or JPQL query. Avoid fetching unnecessary columns or entities. Use DTO projections when only a few fields are required. Also check for the N+1 query problem and use appropriate JOIN FETCH, entity graphs, or batch fetching where necessary.\n\n5️⃣ Reduce the Amount of Data Retrieved\nDon't load thousands of records when the API needs only a small subset. Implement pagination, filtering, and limits using Spring Data's Pageable or database-level limits. This reduces database processing, memory consumption, and network overhead.\n\n6️⃣ Test, Monitor & Validate the Fix\nAfter optimization, compare the before-and-after query execution time and API response time under realistic load. Run load tests and monitor database CPU, connections, query latency, and application metrics. Finally, verify that the optimization improves performance without changing the API's functional behavior.\n\nDo Follow @instagrammer-42 for more tech Content.",
